Z||000014||theogre||03-07-2001||08:08 PM||||There's no real need to remove all the paint. Other than feathering out damaged areas you don't need to go all the way to the pannel. You do need to remove anything loose and break any sign of shinny areas on it. Fine paper on a random orbit sander will make short work of it. On these cars you can start with 400 paper. <P>Once everything is scuffed good, go over it with some 800 or finner wet paper to kill any remaining swirl marks. If you used a random orbit sander you shouldn't have many swirl marks...<P>The hardest part is getting around the trim. Best to take all that off that you can. not only do you get a better sanding job but you prevent any crap in the trim blowing out durring painting. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroJoe||03-07-2001||09:36 PM||||Leaving the paint on acts already as a good primer.  Taking the paint all the way down to the plastic means you will have an incredibly smooth surface for the paint to try to stick to, and you'll probably have to use an epoxy based adhesion coat to get the base paint to stick well.  When we repaint my car, I am only taking it down to the plastic where there are spider web cracks deep into the base, but otherwise the rest of the paint will acts as another coat of primer.<P>Also, when you sand all the way down, you may end up with little valleys in the plastic, where you might have had to work a little harder to get the paint all the way off.  You'd either have to fill these with lacquer putty (3M makes it, like very fine bondo), or use more coats of primer/filler, and then you'd just have to sand even more.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||4
Z||000016||deceler8||03-07-2001||09:42 PM||||If you don't have an expensive stable of tools or an air compressor, invest in a Black & Decker Mouse sander. About $40.<P>It's a very light, versatile sander with velcro backed sandpaper and it's comfortable and quick to use for this purpose.||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||03-07-2001||09:48 PM||||Your rite, theres no better primer than good paint underneath. Most of Lisas is scuffed with scotchbrite and 400 dry. I dont like to have water leaking into the wet paint. Theres a very few places that ill use some filler, DA with 320. Oh yea and have to fix a small break now in pass side ground effects we broke to get fender off.(no one, including service manual told us you had to take off rocker ground effect to get to  2 other rivets.) of course the no good fender we took off didnt break anywhere. Guess well be off to the paint store to get a quart of Indigo Blue and a quart of clear urathane tommorrow. I dont know how much shes gonna have wet sanded and rubbed before Daytona, so dont pick it apart too bad.||||reg||1
